WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.978
Hybrid AI architecture combines local and cloud resources so each workload runs where its requirements are best satisfied.

00:00:08.978 --> 00:00:13.812
Local systems offer control, privacy, and low network latency.

00:00:13.812 --> 00:00:19.566
Cloud GPUs and hosted models offer temporary scale and specialized capability.

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:23.940
Neither environment is inherently correct for every task.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:31.537
Place work through explicit decisions about data sensitivity, latency, model capability, volume, availability, and cost.

00:00:31.537 --> 00:00:44.658
Patterns such as local-first fallback, private local preprocessing, split retrieval and generation, cloud burst jobs, and edge inference are useful only when interfaces and boundaries are documented.

00:00:44.658 --> 00:00:52.254
Fallback must respect policy; some requests should stop instead of moving to an unauthorized route.

00:00:52.254 --> 00:00:58.009
Map data, metadata, logs, caches, and backups across every trust boundary.

00:00:58.009 --> 00:01:06.986
Route only to eligible endpoints, protect credentials, normalize tested API features, and control queues, rate limits, and spending.

00:01:06.986 --> 00:01:13.202
Measure end-to-end user experience and total cost of ownership across both environments.

00:01:13.202 --> 00:01:17.575
Reliability requires tested alternatives, not merely additional components.

00:01:17.575 --> 00:01:21.028
Operate the system as one service.

00:01:21.028 --> 00:01:29.545
Apply identity, segmentation, patching, monitoring, audit, incident response, backup, and rollback to local and cloud components together.

00:01:29.545 --> 00:01:33.459
Review provider risk and changing data flows.

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:43.817
The goal is flexible, controlled AI capability without accidental exposure, runaway cost, or complexity that the team cannot understand and recover.
